# Vercel builds the Next.js app in frontend/, but that build imports three
# shared modules from mobile/src/ (lessonMistakeHints, sentenceConstruction,
# pageCurlGeometry), so the project uploads source files from outside its root
# directory -- which means the whole repository ships with every deployment.
#
# Roughly 1 GB of that is authoring material and a separately-deployed service
# that the web build never reads. Excluding it here is what keeps deployment
# storage under the plan limit; see docs/ for the media pipeline itself.
#
# Anything re-added below must stay reachable from the frontend import graph.
# The build performs no filesystem reads of its own, so only module resolution
# matters when deciding whether a path is safe to exclude.

# Patterns for the four top-level deliverables are anchored with a leading
# slash. Without it these match a directory of that name at ANY depth, which is
# how an unanchored `scripts/` removed frontend/scripts/vercel-ignore-build.sh
# and broke every build with "No such file or directory". A failing ignore
# command errors the deployment rather than falling back to building, so keep
# these anchored.

# Lesson authoring material: source stills, video renders, and slide decks.
# The assets actually served live in frontend/public/lesson-assets.
/Lessons/

# Expo app media. mobile/src/ is deliberately kept whole: it is small, and the
# frontend resolves into it. mobile/src/actionVideos.ts requires files under
# mobile/assets/, but nothing in the web build imports actionVideos, so the
# require is never evaluated and the missing directory cannot break the build.
mobile/assets/
mobile/android/
mobile/ios/
mobile/node_modules/
mobile/tests/

# Python service. Deployed separately on Render (see render.yaml); the frontend
# reaches it over HTTP through lib/api, never from disk.
/backend/

# Documentation, QA manifests, and local tooling.
/docs/
/scripts/

# Lesson stills that ship but are never requested from this origin. The web app
# resolves images through lessonImageSrc (frontend/components/LessonPlayer.js),
# which prefixes getApiBaseUrl(), so every .webp and .png here is fetched from
# the Render backend's /lesson-assets mount instead. These 303 MB were pure
# duplicate weight in the build artifact.
#
# Only the negated poster pattern and the videos below it are served from here,
# so the exclusions are by file extension rather than by directory -- a
# directory exclusion cannot be undone by a later negation.
frontend/public/lesson-assets/*.webp
frontend/public/lesson-assets/*.png
# Videos (*.mp4) are not excluded: mobile's VIDEO_BASE_URL still points here.
# The two-card posters used to be negated back in for lessonTwoCardActionPosterSrc;
# that call site now resolves through mediaUrl, so they are excluded below with
# the rest of the media this origin no longer serves.

# Superseded copy. No code anywhere in the repository references this directory;
# the posters actually used live in lesson-assets under the same names.
frontend/public/lesson-video-posters/

# Everything the web app requests now goes through frontend/lib/mediaUrl.js and
# resolves to the object store, so this origin no longer serves media at all --
# these were shipping in every build and being fetched from nowhere.
#
# *.mp4 is the exception and stays: mobile/src/config.ts hardcodes VIDEO_BASE_URL
# to this origin, and installed apps keep asking for it until those versions age
# out. spanglish-logo.* stays too, being the one asset still referenced
# root-relative rather than through the resolver.
#
# The cost of removing these is the rollback: with NEXT_PUBLIC_MEDIA_BASE_URL
# unset, mediaUrl falls back to root-relative paths that will now 404. Unsetting
# it is no longer a complete undo -- restoring these three lines is part of it.
frontend/public/audio-cache/
frontend/public/sfx/
frontend/public/lesson-assets/*-two-card-poster.webp

# Local generated output. Neither is tracked, so a Git-integrated build never
# sees them, but `vercel deploy` from a workstation uploads the working
# directory -- which here would mean 2.2 GB of tmp/ and 480 MB of output/.
# tmp/ is gitignored and was still listed here, so gitignore alone was never
# treated as protection against a CLI deploy. The agent worktrees are 12 GB and
# 734 MB -- five times the two directories this section already defended -- and
# were missing by oversight rather than by decision.
tmp/
output/
__pycache__/
venv/
.codex-release-worktrees/
.codex-task-worktrees/
.codex-remote-attachments/
.agents/
